    UPDATE events
    SET
        description = 'Title : Semiconductor Grafting: A Scalable Approach to Lattice-Mismatched Heterojunctions Abstract : Semiconductor heterojunctions have revolutionized modern electronics, optoelectronics, and photonics. Traditionally, their fabrication via epitaxy or heteroepitaxy requires strict lattice matching, limiting material choices and device architectures. While lattice-matched heterojunctions have driven significant technological progress, integrating lattice-mismatched semiconductors while preserving their crystallinity remains an unsolved challenge. Overcoming this limitation could open new frontiers in materials engineering and device innovation. In this talk, I will introduce semiconductor grafting, a novel and practical framework for forming near-abrupt, electrically active heterojunctions between lattice-mismatched materials. This approach exploits quantum tunneling mechanisms to facilitate efficient charge transport while maintaining the intrinsic order of each semiconductor and minimizing interface defect states. I will then demonstrate the scalability of this technique by fabricating and characterizing wafer-scale (≥2-inch) grafted heterojunctions that exhibit high throughput, uniformity, and performance. Finally, I will highlight their application in advanced electronic, optoelectronic, and photonic devices, illustrating their potential for next-generation semiconductor technologies. Speakers Dr. Jie Zhou',
